   <div class="ev panel"><div class="time">8:45 AM</div><div>
    <div class="ttl">Panel 1: Cancer-Related Dysbiosis, Point-of-Care Diagnostics &amp; Biomarkers</div>
    <div class="lbl">Consensus</div>
    <ul><li>Microbiome features have demonstrated potential as biomarkers of cancer treatment response and toxicity.</li><li>Standardized sampling, analysis, and prospective clinical validation are required before microbiome diagnostics can be incorporated into routine oncology practice.</li></ul>
    <div class="lbl">Key debate</div><ul><li>Are microbiome diagnostic tools ready to enter routine oncology practice?</li></ul>
    <div class="lbl">Speakers and topics <span class="dim">(7–10 min each)</span></div>
    <ul class="spk">
     <li><b>Nicola Segata</b> — Applications of metagenomics sequencing in the oncology clinic. Should we be offering metagenomics testing to all oncology patients?</li>
     <li><b>Laurence Zitvogel</b> — Is dysbiosis itself a clinically actionable biomarker in patients with ICI? Validation of TOPOSCORE across cohorts.</li>
     <li><b>Susan Bullman</b> — Next frontier: microbiome for cancer initiation, progression, and screening/early detection. Should we routinely test for Fusobacteria in the context of CRC screening?</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="lbl">Moderators</div><ul><li>Marty Blaser*</li><li>Pamela Ohashi</li></ul>
    <div class="lbl">Additional participants joining on stage</div><ul><li>Andrew Almonte</li><li>Gianmarco Piccinno</li><li>Nazli Dizman</li><li>Robert Jenq</li></ul>
    <div class="lbl">Structured discussion — 60 minutes</div>
    <ul><li>What constitutes a clinically actionable microbiome biomarker?</li><li>Should microbiome testing enter clinical trials now?</li><li>Should microbiome testing enter routine oncology practice for prognostication, treatment selection, clinical trial stratification, and/or early cancer detection?</li><li>What level of validation is sufficient?</li><li>Stool, plasma metabolites, tumor microbiome—or all three?</li><li>What is needed to move these tools from diagnosis to early detection or prevention?</li></ul>
    <div class="lbl">Expected deliverables — consensus recommendations on</div>
    <ul><li>Standards for microbiome sampling and diagnostics</li><li>Evidence required for implementation of microbiome diagnostics into routine oncology practice</li><li>Highest-priority validation studies</li></ul>
   </div></div>

   <div class="ev brk"><div class="time">10:15 AM</div><div><div class="ttl">Break</div><div class="loc">15 minutes</div></div></div>

   <div class="ev panel key"><div class="time">10:30 AM</div><div>
    <span class="pill">★ YOUR KEY PRESENTATION</span>
    <div class="ttl">Panel 2: First Line of Defense: Antibiotic Stewardship</div>
    <div class="lbl">Consensus</div>
    <ul><li>Antibiotics exposure before, during, and after ICI, CAR-T, BMT are associated with poor survival.</li><li>Antibiotic stewardship should be practiced prior to and during these therapies.</li></ul>
    <div class="lbl">Key debates</div>
    <ul><li>Which interventions should be prioritized: stewardship, protection, or restoration?</li><li>Should therapies be delayed in the setting of recent antibiotic exposure?</li></ul>
    <div class="lbl">Speakers and topics <span class="dim">(7–10 min each)</span></div>
    <ul class="spk">
     <li><b>Susan Seo</b> — Roadmap to implementation of ATB stewardship in oncology (in-patient and especially out-patient). Who is responsible for ATB stewardship?</li>
     <li><b>Gianluca Ianiro</b> — Microbiome preservation after ATBs: clinical trial update.</li>
     <li style="background:#fff3cf;border-radius:4px;padding:4px 8px;list-style:none;margin-left:-8px"><b>★ Marco Ruella</b> — “Importance of ATB stewardship in CAR-T cell therapy: from first discovery to the clinic”</li>
     <li><b>Melody Smith</b> — ATB stewardship in CAR-T context: lessons learned from shifting practice at Stanford.</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="lbl">Moderators</div><ul><li>Cliff McDonald*</li><li>Elisabeth de Vries</li></ul>
    <div class="lbl">Additional participant joining on stage</div><ul><li>Lisa Derosa</li></ul>
    <div class="lbl">Structured discussion — 50 minutes</div>
    <ul><li>Should stewardship become part of ICI guidelines now?</li><li>Which patients truly need antibiotics despite microbiome concerns?</li><li>What randomized trial would definitively answer these questions?</li><li>What can we do to manage ATB stewardship in outpatients?</li><li>How can we scale antibiotic stewardship principles across different practice settings, beyond ID/pharmacology?</li><li>What can we do when antibiotics are administered?</li><li>What is the role of public health agencies in aligning standard of care with these recommendations and what do they need from this body?</li></ul>
    <div class="lbl">Expected deliverables — consensus recommendations on</div>
    <ul><li>Draft stewardship recommendations</li><li>Priority interventional trials</li></ul>
   </div></div>

   <div class="ev brk"><div class="time">12:00 PM</div><div><div class="ttl">Lunch and Group Photo</div><div class="loc">Location: Swiss Re Foyer</div></div></div>

   <h3>Afternoon session <span class="dim" style="font-size:.85rem;font-weight:400;color:var(--gray)">— all panels in Swiss Re Forum A unless otherwise stated</span></h3>

   <div class="ev panel"><div class="time">1:00 PM</div><div>
    <div class="ttl">Panel 3: Role of Co-Medications on Dysbiosis and Response to Cancer Therapy</div>
    <div class="loc">Location: Swiss Re Forum A</div>
    <div class="lbl">Consensus</div><ul><li>Co-medications should be reviewed at every medical visit […and unnecessary medications that are deleterious for the microbiome should be stopped or replaced].</li></ul>
    <div class="lbl">Key debate</div><ul><li>Should unnecessary co-medications be reviewed and stopped prior to initiation of immunotherapy?</li></ul>
    <div class="lbl">Speakers and topics <span class="dim">(7–10 min each)</span></div>
    <ul class="spk"><li><b>David Pinato</b> — Co-medications and immunotherapy: association, causality, or actionable target?</li><li><b>Monika Krzyzanowska</b> — How to implement quality-of-care changes at the hospital level?</li></ul>
    <div class="lbl">Moderators</div><ul><li>Bertrand Routy*</li><li>Tsoni Peled</li></ul>
    <div class="lbl">Structured discussion — 45 minutes</div>
    <ul><li>Should PPIs (or other co-medications) be stopped if they are not clinically indicated?</li><li>Which medications deserve routine reporting?</li><li>Which should become stratification variables in microbiome trials or studies?</li><li>If data are lacking, what should we prioritize? What do current data suggest are the most urgent co-medications to investigate? Which truly require randomized trials?</li></ul>
    <div class="lbl">Expected deliverables — consensus recommendations on</div>
    <ul><li>A core list of co-medications that should be prioritized and systematically captured in immunotherapy and microbiome studies</li><li>Which co-medications should be included as covariates or stratification factors in future clinical trials</li><li>Minimum standards for reporting timing, dose, duration, and indication of co-medication exposure</li><li>Priority co-medications for prospective mechanistic and interventional studies</li><li>Whether routine medication review and deprescribing should be incorporated into oncology care before and during immunotherapy</li></ul>
   </div></div>

   <div class="ev brk"><div class="time">2:05 PM</div><div><div class="ttl">Break</div><div class="loc">10 minutes</div></div></div>

   <div class="ev panel"><div class="time">2:15 PM</div><div>
    <div class="ttl">Panel 4: Nutrition and Prebiotics <span class="dim">(combined panel)</span></div>
    <div class="loc">Location: Swiss Re Forum A</div>
    <div class="lbl">Consensus</div>
    <ul><li>Diet is a major modifiable determinant of gut microbiome composition and function and should be systematically captured in microbiome-centered oncology studies.</li><li>Dietary interventions and prebiotics represent attractive, low-risk strategies to modulate the gut microbiome, but prospective evidence demonstrating improved cancer treatment outcomes remains insufficient for microbiome-directed recommendations as standard oncology care.</li></ul>
    <div class="lbl">Key debates</div>
    <ul><li>Can diet or prebiotics become a microbiome therapies?</li><li>Does the risk/benefit profile of recommending high-fiber, minimally processed diets suggest we should act with more urgency to recommend dietary changes to patients?</li><li>What is the role of medical foods within the current regulatory landscape and is there a roll for their study as part of RCTs?</li></ul>
    <div class="lbl">Speakers and topics <span class="dim">(7–10 min each)</span></div>
    <ul class="spk">
     <li><b>Wendy Garrett</b> — Feeding the microbiome: what biology should guide dietary and prebiotic interventions?</li>
     <li><b>Urvi Shah</b> — The next generation of diet interventions in immuno-oncology.</li>
     <li><b>Joseph Tintelnot</b> — Updates from a diet interventional trial: lessons learned and recommendations for microbiome trial standardization.</li>
     <li><b>Jen McQuade</b> — Are we ready to prescribe diet alongside immunotherapy?</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="lbl">Moderators</div><ul><li>Kathy McCoy*</li><li>Sandrine Aspeslagh*</li><li>Jens Walter</li></ul>
    <div class="lbl">Additional participant joining on stage</div><ul><li>Luigi Nezi</li></ul>
    <div class="lbl">Structured discussion — 60 minutes</div>
    <ul><li>Can dietary interventions improve immunotherapy?</li><li>Why might different nutritional regimens / prebiotics have a common effect on immunotherapy outcomes?</li><li>Should every microbiome trial make any recommendations on patients not making changes to their diet on the protocol? How should diet best be controlled for?</li><li>How do we improve adherence/make dietary interventions more feasible?</li><li>Is providing meals scalable? How do we increase scalability at institutional and regional levels?</li><li>What dietary intervention has the strongest evidence today?</li></ul>
    <div class="lbl">Expected deliverables — consensus recommendations on</div>
    <ul><li>Minimum dietary reporting standards</li><li>Priority dietary intervention trials</li><li>A consensus perspective answering: what is actionable advice to deliver to patients today?</li></ul>
   </div></div>

   <div class="ev brk"><div class="time">4:00 PM</div><div><div class="ttl">Break</div><div class="loc">10 minutes</div></div></div>

   <div class="ev panel"><div class="time">4:10 PM</div><div>
    <div class="ttl">Panel 5: FMT to Rational Consortia</div>
    <div class="loc">Location: Swiss Re Forum A · FMT = fecal microbiota transplantation</div>
    <div class="lbl">Consensus</div><ul><li>FMT is a discovery tool but has challenges for long-term commercial utility.</li></ul>
    <div class="lbl">Key debates</div>
    <ul><li>Are FMT trials designed to help us understand what might make a rationally designed consortium more likely to succeed? Should we keep doing FMT trials?</li><li>How can LBPs replace FMT as therapeutics? Is there a “one size fits most” therapeutic for/beyond cancer?</li></ul>
    <div class="lbl">Speakers and topics <span class="dim">(7–10 min each)</span></div>
    <ul class="spk">
     <li><b>Gene Chang</b> — Ecological perspective: engraftment, mechanisms, donor effects, what FMT has taught the field.</li>
