 <details class="day" open id="day3">
  <summary><span>Friday, November 20, 2026</span><span style="font-weight:400;font-size:.9rem">Half-day session · Panel 6 · Shuttles</span></summary>
  <div class="daybody">
   <h3>Morning session</h3>
   <div class="ev"><div class="time">7:30 AM</div><div><div class="ttl">Breakfast opens</div><div class="loc">Location: Swiss Re Foyer</div></div></div>
   <div class="ev"><div class="time">8:30 AM</div><div><div class="ttl">Welcome and Agenda Review</div><div class="loc">Location: Swiss Re Forum A</div>
    <ul><li>Nadim Ajami — MD Anderson Cancer Center</li><li>Arielle Elkrief — CHUM</li></ul></div></div>

   <div class="ev panel"><div class="time">8:45 AM</div><div>
    <div class="ttl">Panel 6: Clinical Trials</div>
    <div class="loc">Location: Swiss Re Forum A</div>
    <div class="lbl">Consensus (extracted from current manuscript)</div>
    <ol>
     <li>Antibiotic exposure within 30 days of ICI initiation reduces ICI benefit and is a stratification variable.</li>
     <li>FMT is a discovery and proof-of-concept tool, not a scalable therapeutic product. Development of any clinically successful FMT signal should transition to a manufacturable full-spectrum microbiota community or defined-bacterial consortia.</li>
     <li>Indication selection should favor settings with intact immune priming (1L metastatic, neoadjuvant) over later-line and adjuvant settings, where mechanisms of resistance are layered.</li>
     <li>Phase II microbiome trials in solid tumor oncology should use landmark survival as the primary efficacy endpoint, not RECIST 1.1 ORR.</li>
     <li>Every microbiome trial must collect a defined minimum biospecimen set (stool, plasma, serum, PBMC, germline DNA, tumor where feasible) at protocol-mandated timepoints under a single central SOP.</li>
     <li>Microbiome trials should capture concomitant antibiotic exposure, PPI/H2RA use, validated dietary intake, probiotic use, recent travel and hospitalization on the electronic case report form (eCRF).</li>
     <li>SER-155 BTD and Vowst approval set the regulatory bar: microbiome products are biologics and require CMC packages comparable to other live biologics.</li>
    </ol>
    <div class="lbl">Key debates</div>
    <ul><li>What will it take to move from promising early-phase microbiome trials to a definitive practice-changing trial?</li><li>What have we learned from negative or unsuccessful late-stage microbiome trials outside oncology?</li><li>Should the next generation of trials enroll all patients or enrich for patients with a microbiome-defined phenotype?</li><li>Do we need to demonstrate microbiome modulation/engraftment as a pharmacodynamic endpoint, or is improvement in a clinical endpoint sufficient?</li></ul>
    <div class="lbl">Speakers and topics <span class="dim">(7–10 min each)</span></div>
    <ul class="spk">
     <li><b>Caroline Robert</b> — From clinical trial to standard of care: what makes a trial practice-changing? Perspective: having led multiple melanoma trials that changed clinical practice, what can microbiome oncology learn from successful therapeutic development?</li>
     <li><b>Pippa Corrie</b> — From promising signal to definitive trial: what evidence is enough to move forward?</li>
     <li><b>Diwakar Davar</b> — The next great microbiome oncology trial. Part II of Meeting 1: what have we learned since last year, and which trial-design problems continue to hold the field back? “What mistakes are we repeating?”</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="lbl">Moderators</div>
    <ul><li>Giorgio Trinchieri</li><li>Pierre Olivier Gaudreau*</li><li>Oriana Miltiadous <span class="dim">(also authoring consensus statements, research priorities and evidence standards for the separate pediatrics section)</span></li></ul>
    <div class="lbl">Structured discussion — 60 minutes</div>
    <ul><li>What clinical and microbiome endpoints should be mandatory: engraftment, ecological remodeling, functional changes, metabolomics?</li><li>How should diet, antibiotics, co-medications, geography, and baseline microbiome heterogeneity be incorporated into trial design?</li><li>What have previous negative late-stage microbiome trials taught us?</li><li>What evidence would convince oncologists and guideline committees to change practice?</li><li>How important is it to incorporate microbiome measurements in non-microbiome trials? (Particularly in phase 0/I trials of novel IO drugs and combinations)</li></ul>
    <div class="lbl">Expected deliverables — consensus recommendations on</div>
    <ul><li>Core clinical endpoints and minimum microbiome/pharmacodynamic endpoints for future trials</li><li>When and how microbiome biomarkers should be used for patient selection or stratification</li><li>Minimum standards for controlling or capturing diet, antibiotics, co-medications, geography, and other microbiome-modifying exposures</li></ul>
   </div></div>

   <div class="ev brk"><div class="time">10:15 AM</div><div><div class="ttl">Break and Small-Group Work</div><div class="loc">Small groups revise consensus statements</div></div></div>

